{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.740.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.090.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.740$ to $0.090.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.740}{0.090}=8.222….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈8.222… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(8.222…) \\\\ p & ≈1.917….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =2(0.6)^{4}-(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.129\\,6-1\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.259\\,2-0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.181\\,44 \\\\  & ≈0.181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =2(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.003\\,2-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.002\\,88 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.181\\,44$ to $0.002\\,88.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.181\\,44}{0.002\\,88}=63≈63.000.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈63 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(63) \\\\ p & ≈3.771….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.003"] ["math" "0.042"] ["math" "0.094"] ["math" "0.181"] ["math" "0.257"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.181"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.003"] ["math" "0.042"] ["math" "0.094"] ["math" "0.181"] ["math" "0.257"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.003"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "27.000"] ["math" "36.000"] ["math" "63.000"] ["math" "81.000"] ["math" "9.000"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "63.000"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334567" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.7$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.7) & =5(0.7)^{4}-(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.2401-1\\cdot 0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.200\\,5-0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.032\\,43 \\\\  & ≈1.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.007\\,68 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.7$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.7}{0.2}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}=(3.5)^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.032\\,43$ to $0.007\\,68.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.032\\,43}{0.007\\,68}=134.430\\,99…≈134.431.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p} & ≈134.430\\,99… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(134.430\\,99…) \\\\ p & ≈3.912….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.008"] ["math" "0.207"] ["math" "0.321"] ["math" "0.514"] ["math" "1.032"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.032"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a4