nswer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334569" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.310.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.078.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.310$ to $0.078.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.310}{0.078}=3.974….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.974… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.974…) \\\\ p & ≈1.990….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"quest