e/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "102.385"] ["math" "104.632"] ["math" "107.403"] ["math" "120.050"] ["math" "99.068"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "104.632"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334724" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =7(0.5)^{4}-2(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0.062\\,5-2\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =7(0.25)^{4}-2(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (0.003\\,906…)-2\\cdot (0.000\\,976…) \\\\  & =0.025\\,39… \\\\  & ≈0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.375$ to $0.025\\,39….$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.375}{(0.025\\,39…)}=14.769\\,23…≈14.769.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈14.769\\,23… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(14.769\\,23…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.884… \\\\ p & ≈2.884….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.025"] ["math" "0.075"] ["math" "0.225"] ["math" "0.375"] ["math" "0.550"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/quest