 "value" [["math" "12.135"] ["math" "14.769"] ["math" "17.671"] ["math" "7.864"] ["math" "9.168"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14.769"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333706" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9}$\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{6}=2(Δx)^{5+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{4}=0.4(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{5}=6(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3Δx.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is  $9(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-333692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-333693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-333693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.8(Δx)^{2}=0.8(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{4}=9(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.18$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.18)≈0.2025.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.04,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.04)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.04}=4.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(4.5)^{2}=20.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.04,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.04)≈\\frac{0.2025}{20.25}=0.01.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334827" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334827" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.01"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.15$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.15)≈0.0225.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.10}=1.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(1.5)^{2}=2.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.0225}{2.25}=0.01.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334796" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334796" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.01"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334781" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334781" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.002"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.108.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2+1}={3}^{3}=27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.108}{27}=0.004.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334948" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334948" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.004"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "975ed56ff6c5a23efea6667ec0c4ef6cd62aedfc9702f7e61a9ad1f1717bb1dd"} {"question" "q-334946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "q-334946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.05"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2385f80aa98497014e0a45dfe2cfa1a1786dae36b112f1f7b2f74ac30170ba2"} {"question" "e-21684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/ee7115883733062534307c8ff2b1795c77d47d1e8dffeb357dcbe94e07e1f400.png)\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/3883232317dc9ab3e8888fab646fb593ca37585ef3367c5b3000be226962e512.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/ea99ff6ac18f5bb8506edcecfe913b338b64d04e99532f67cec6eb94efc2e9fa.png)\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/d8eaeb8b6abf175f486560cf0d407fd97cd5180907c2db8e6436ccc7e4b09f04.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21684.json" "file_sha256" "7080819cb88234208cf10a3d47f7a5f9f05befd5e51ce697c46a0831a692ceac"} {"question" "e-21684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =4(0.4)^{3}+(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.064+1\\cdot 0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.256+0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.281\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.282.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+1\\cdot 0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.032+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 2^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈8.380\\,952… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(8.380\\,95…) \\\\ p & ≈3.067….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21684.json" "file_sha256" "7080819cb88234208cf10a3d47f7a5f9f05befd5e51ce697c46a0831a692ceac"} {"question" "e-21685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21685.json" "file_sha256" "6824b7b2c4dc7cdc33d7e1d44247f2980961655bf79760116ce811cfa19d681e"} {"question" "e-21685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.987… \\\\ p & ≈2.987….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21685.json" "file_sha256" "6824b7b2c4dc7cdc33d7e1d44247f2980961655bf79760116ce811cfa19d681e"} {"question" "e-21686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n\n- $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21686.json" "file_sha256" "bf16f1634dd029d6e9874576f9ebb88bab17d9897ee526f27e73267901f9f136"} {"question" "e-21686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21686.json" "file_sha256" "bf16f1634dd029d6e9874576f9ebb88bab17d9897ee526f27e73267901f9f136"} {"question" "e-21687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24.$\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/31f454d50de05da419a55dfcdd26ac8d7883635e6eb0b95367f9f80fb30643a5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21687.json" "file_sha256" "083876f464e737dd75404d19cead4cae92bc4db3ccae7255e3c1e77bd00bbc82"} {"question" "e-21687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/example-21687.json" "file_sha256" "083876f464e737dd75404d19cead4cae92bc4db3ccae7255e3c1e77bd00bbc82"} {"question" "q-333702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "150a27808b6d843510b8faab6affa4730c2caed4487d740cd78056be6a200129" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "The step size decreases by 0.08/0.02=4 and the local truncation error decreases by 0.13/0.002=65. For a method of order p, LTE scales as h^(p+1), so p≈log(65)/log(4)−1≈2.01118, giving estimated integer order 2. Original option c (zero-based index 2), the authentic worked solution, and successful grading agree. Stored 3 confuses the LTE exponent p+1 with the method order p; 3 and 2 must remain distinct." "bindings" {"problem" "8223cb39f41e01ed1c8bf2255ca640eabd08105eba072fc4d2ccf48a6411256d" "worked_solution" "f9ade1c7551f6d28a0fdd0feb10b7254ca72b0074cc3a7491acb63b1f3cf6cf3" "original_field" "eb6971e0913cd300f53d7b149e67d23d759d8c8305e62348738a240559cd2576" "reviewed_field" "42fc2103574ca2965de0674b82cf91876e2c3d9b88acc4313ab493945e373d54" "raw_widget" "26f7b38ecd4183070ad876fd8fb082ab405184a6cd01d2dcc26cf8a4a242e943"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-333702-before.json" "sha256" "0b0ba3cfd958c844ba4c8fb5eff9e0ed840dd54d4ea3c3b6cd93c918d04e5b77"} {"path" "q-333702-after.png" "sha256" "63da0657e7276914b1e74b4afb13df8c8eb32a4522a96f21967f9ecaaf3e54fd"} {"path" "history-q-333702.json" "sha256" "837fd2fcf77403fbd6106787aed20250b1810e6878a3cbeef5e1b98a9a07229c"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "5e0e422c5ec5d26404e6ae7ebde30630e6ba954181f9c1ff5ce0ba398f2cf9de"}]} {"question" "q-333702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "150a27808b6d843510b8faab6affa4730c2caed4487d740cd78056be6a200129" "rationale" "The step size decreases by 0.08/0.02=4 and the local truncation error decreases by 0.13/0.002=65. For a method of order p, LTE scales as h^(p+1), so p≈log(65)/log(4)−1≈2.01118, giving estimated integer order 2. Original option c (zero-based index 2), the authentic worked solution, and successful grading agree. Stored 3 confuses the LTE exponent p+1 with the method order p; 3 and 2 must remain distinct."}] "usage" {"q-333702" [] "e-21686" [] "e-21687" [] "q-333692" [] "e-21684" [] "e-21685" [] "q-333706" []} "basis" 1716 "retractions" [[17592186221922 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08) = 0.13$.\n- With step size $Δx = 0.02$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02) = 0.002$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method."] [17592186221922 :question/answer-fields 17592186220473] [17592186221923 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n$τ(Δx) = 7(Δx)^{4} - 2(Δx)^{5}$.\nComplete the following sentences. With step size $Δx = 0.5$, the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nWith step size $Δx = 0.25$, the local truncation error is $\\approx$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nDecreasing from $Δx = 0.5$ to $Δx = 0.25$, the LTE is reduced by a factor of $\\approx$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAccording to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186221918 :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{2}$\n2. $e(Δx) = 0.3Δx + 2(Δx)^{2}$\n3. $e(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{2} + 4(Δx)^{5}$"] [17592186221125 :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n$e(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4$.\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$, the global truncation error is $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the global truncation error is $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the GTE is reduced by a factor of $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n$XXX$"] [17592186221125 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p$.\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$, the GTE is\n$e(0.4)|= 4(0.4)^{3} + (0.4)^{4}; = 4 \\cdot 0.064 + 1 \\cdot 0.0256; = 0.256 + 0.0256; = 0.2816; \\approx 0.282$.\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the GTE is\n$e(0.2)|= 4(0.2)^{3} + (0.2)^{4}; = 4 \\cdot 0.008 + 1 \\cdot 0.0016; = 0.032 + 0.0016; = 0.0336; \\approx 0.034$.\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p$, the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n$k^p = 2^p$.\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{p} & \\approx 8.380952… \\\\ p & \\approx \\log_{2} (8.38095…) \\\\ p & \\approx 3.067…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}$.\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad e(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3$."] [17592186221126 :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24$, the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8$.\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer."] [17592186221126 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}$.\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p$, the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3^{p + 1} & \\approx 80 \\\\ p + 1 & \\approx \\log_{3} (80) \\\\ p + 1 & \\approx 3.987… \\\\ p & \\approx 2.987…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3$."] [17592186221127 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n2. $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n3. $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$"] [17592186221127 :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n$e(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0$,\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n$\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0$,\n\nthen the method is of order $p$.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2$.\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}$.\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4$.\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [17592186221128 :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1$.\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24$.\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1$, what LTE should we expect?\n\n$XXX$"] [17592186221128 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}$.\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1$, the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.1\n$$\n\nthe expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220460 17592186220461 17592186220462 17592186220463 17592186220464 17592186220474 17592186220475 17592186220476 17592186220477 17592186220478 17592186220480 17592186220481 17592186220482 17592186220483 17592186220484 17592186220486 17592186220487 17592186220488 17592186220489 17592186220490 17592186220492 17592186220493 17592186220494 17592186220495 17592186220496 17592186220498 17592186220499 17592186220500 17592186220501 17592186220502] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220459 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220459 :answer-field/id] [17592186220459 :answer-field/key] [17592186220459 :answer-field/type] [17592186220473 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220473 :answer-field/id] [17592186220473 :answer-field/key] [17592186220473 :answer-field/type] [17592186220479 :answer-field/id] [17592186220479 :answer-field/key] [17592186220479 :answer-field/type] [17592186220485 :answer-field/id] [17592186220485 :answer-field/key] [17592186220485 :answer-field/type] [17592186220491 :answer-field/id] [17592186220491 :answer-field/key] [17592186220491 :answer-field/type] [17592186220497 :answer-field/id] [17592186220497 